Avalanche risk modeling for swiss regions

==Project Description==

This project implements a predictive model for avalanche danger levels in the Swiss Alps using:

daily meteorological measurements from MeteoSwiss weather stations, and

official SLF avalanche danger indices.

The pipeline performs:

loading of meteorological data for any MeteoSwiss station,

matching it with SLF avalanche danger data for a selected prediction region,

full data cleaning and temporal alignment,

training of a Weighted Least Squares (WLS) regression model, with increased emphasis on high-risk days,

automatic generation of a C function that computes avalanche risk for the chosen station,

export of all graphs and evaluation metrics to the results/ directory.

The code is designed to be reproducible, portable, and executable on any machine with MATLAB installed.


==Input files (located in /data/)==

The data/ folder contains:

MeteoSwiss meteorological datasets in .csv format
(e.g., meteo_weissfluhjoch.csv, meteo_pilatus.csv, etc.)

SLF avalanche danger dataset:
risk_index_slf.csv

All datasets used in this project are publicly available from:

https://www.meteosuisse.admin.ch

https://www.envidat.ch

Users may add new stations simply by placing additional .csv files in data/


==Output files (generated in /results/)==

Upon execution, the program automatically creates the following outputs in results/:

all generated figures (.png and .pdf formats)

tables of model performance metrics

predictions for the selected station

a C source file (risk_function.c) containing a fully standalone risk-prediction routine

No output is pre-stored in the repository: every file is generated automatically at runtime

==Running the program==
Dependencies

This project has been tested with the following configuration:

Operating system: Linux (mint)

MATLAB: R2025a 

gcc for C functions compiling
================================= attention à ca The executable or shared object file can be placed in the "bin/" directory.============================


Execute

To run the entire pipeline — preprocessing, model training, hyperparameter selection, graph generation, and C code export — simply execute:

matlab -batch "run('src/Forecast_version_model.m')"

All output files will automatically be written into the results/ directory.Contributors

Authors of the code: Pablo Montani 
